Scientific Opportunity Discovery Under Partial Identification

A First-Principles Framework for Discovering Research Questions from Structured Knowledge

Second Draft — Version 0.2

Ouadi Maakoul
Human + AI Collective Research Draft

Abstract

Scientific research does not begin with a thesis. It begins with a state of incomplete knowledge.

A scientific archive contains observations, models, claims, assumptions, contradictions, unresolved questions, and regions in which multiple explanations remain compatible with the available evidence. Existing approaches to scientific discovery provide important tools for literature-based discovery, knowledge-graph construction, contradiction detection, active learning, experimental design, partial identification, information-theoretic inference, and value-of-information analysis. However, these approaches do not by themselves provide a general mathematical representation of the transition from an existing body of knowledge to a candidate research opportunity.

This draft develops a first-principles formulation of that problem.

The central proposal is to represent an archive \mathcal A by an admissible model space

\mathcal M(\mathcal A)\subseteq\Theta,

where \Theta is a space of possible models, mechanisms, parameterizations, or explanations and \mathcal M(\mathcal A) contains those compatible with the currently accepted constraints extracted from the archive.

Under this representation, unresolvedness is not identified exclusively with contradiction. Three distinct structures are separated:

\boxed{\text{Contradiction} \neq \text{Underdetermination} \neq \text{Research Opportunity}.}

A contradiction concerns incompatible constraints. Underdetermination concerns the persistence of multiple admissible models. A research opportunity is associated with a possible observation, intervention, measurement, or theoretical constraint capable of refining the unresolved structure.

For a candidate c, a possible observation Y_c induces a family of refined model spaces

\mathcal M_{c,y} = \{\theta \in \mathcal M : \theta \text{ is compatible with } Y_c = y\}.

The fundamental mathematical phenomenon is therefore model-space refinement.

The first theorem establishes that if two models are observationally equivalent under the current observation family but distinguishable under a candidate observation, then the candidate strictly refines the corresponding observational equivalence class.

Information-theoretic quantities such as conditional mutual information and robust information bounds are subsequently introduced as candidate measures of discrimination or identification gain, rather than assumed a priori to define scientific value.

Version 0.2 adds a formal kill operator, a Pareto order over candidates, an epistemic-status and provenance layer for the archive, and a correction to the interpretation of robust information bounds. The draft remains conservative. It does not claim that model-space refinement constitutes a complete theory of scientific discovery.

Keywords: research opportunity, partial identification, observational equivalence, model-space refinement, robust information, Pareto order, kill operator, epistemic status.

---

1. Introduction

A common way of constructing a theoretical research project is:

\text{idea} \rightarrow \text{literature} \rightarrow \text{gap} \rightarrow \text{formalization} \rightarrow \text{critique} \rightarrow \text{new gap}.

This procedure is useful for validation. It is less obviously suitable for discovery. Repeated application can produce increasingly sophisticated formulations without necessarily identifying the underlying research problem. Each iteration may reveal another missing assumption, another mathematical refinement, another literature connection, or another possible theorem, while the fundamental question remains unresolved.

The present work begins from a different direction. Rather than asking immediately What thesis should be written?, we ask What mathematical structure represents something that remains genuinely unresolved in an existing body of scientific knowledge?

The distinction is important. A literature archive is not merely a collection of papers. It contains constraints on a space of possible explanations. If the archive is sufficiently informative, only a small region of the model space may remain admissible. If it is incomplete, multiple models may remain possible. If observations cannot distinguish some models, observational equivalence arises. If a contradiction exists, some subset of the extracted constraints may be mutually incompatible.

The research-discovery problem can therefore be reformulated as:

\boxed{\text{Given a knowledge state, identify unresolved structure and possible operations that refine it.}}

This formulation provides a common mathematical language for several existing research traditions: partial identification, statistical identifiability, active learning, optimal experimental design, model discrimination, information theory, value of information, contradiction detection, knowledge-graph reasoning, literature-based discovery, formal epistemology, and scientific hypothesis generation. The purpose of this draft is not to claim that these fields are equivalent. The purpose is to determine whether a common primitive can connect them without collapsing important distinctions.

---

2. The Scientific Archive as a Knowledge State

Let \mathcal A=\{d_1,d_2,\ldots,d_n\} denote a scientific archive. The elements d_i may represent papers, datasets, experimental results, theoretical propositions, measurements, formal derivations, negative results, or other scientifically relevant documents.

The archive itself is not yet a mathematical knowledge state. We introduce an extraction operator

E:\mathcal A\rightarrow K,

where K is a structured collection of scientific constraints:

K(\mathcal A) = \{k_1,k_2,\ldots,k_m\}.

Each k_j may be represented as a logical, statistical, mathematical, causal, empirical, or methodological constraint. The extraction process is not assumed to be perfect. This distinction is important because the proposed framework concerns the structure induced by the available knowledge, not the claim that automated extraction has solved scientific semantics.

2.1 Epistemic Status [0.2]

Each extracted constraint k_j carries an epistemic status

\sigma(k_j) \in \{\text{verified}, \text{supported}, \text{hypothetical}, \text{speculative}, \text{contradicted}, \text{killed}, \text{unresolved}\}.

The status propagates through derivation: if k_j is derived from \{k_{j_1}, \ldots, k_{j_r}\}, then

\sigma(k_j) \preceq \min_{i} \sigma(k_{j_i}),

under the order

\text{verified} \succ \text{supported} \succ \text{hypothetical} \succ \text{speculative}.

The knowledge state \mathcal M(\mathcal A) should be computed only from constraints with status at least supported, unless the candidate generation step explicitly targets hypothetical or speculative structures. Without this layer, unsupported LLM output silently becomes "knowledge" and the framework loses auditability.

2.2 Provenance [0.2]

Every constraint k_j retains a provenance set

\operatorname{prov}(k_j) \subseteq \mathcal A,

and every derived candidate c retains a provenance set

\operatorname{prov}(c) = \bigcup_{j \in \operatorname{dep}(c)} \operatorname{prov}(k_j).

The system must be able to answer, for any candidate: which documents and which claims produced this candidate? Without provenance, a candidate is not scientifically auditable. Provenance is not optional decoration. It is a precondition for the framework to be scientifically usable.

---

3. The Space of Admissible Models

Let \Theta denote a model space. Depending on the scientific domain, an element \theta\in\Theta may represent a parameter vector, a probability distribution, a causal model, a dynamical system, a physical mechanism, a geological history, a biological process, an algorithm, or another structured explanation.

Each extracted constraint k_j defines a subset C_j\subseteq\Theta containing models compatible with that constraint. The admissible model space induced by the archive is

\boxed{\mathcal M(\mathcal A) = \bigcap_{j=1}^{m} C_j.}

Abbreviate \mathcal M = \mathcal M(\mathcal A). The mapping

\boxed{\mathcal A \longrightarrow K(\mathcal A) \longrightarrow \mathcal M(\mathcal A)}

is the first mathematical backbone of the framework.

---

4. Knowledge as a Shrinking Model Space

Suppose successive scientific results add constraints C_1,C_2,\ldots,C_t. Then the corresponding knowledge states satisfy

\mathcal M_0 \supseteq \mathcal M_1 \supseteq \cdots \supseteq \mathcal M_t,

where \mathcal M_t = \bigcap_{j=1}^{t} C_j. This provides a useful operational interpretation of scientific progress:

\boxed{\text{new knowledge} \Rightarrow \text{constraint on admissible model space}.}

In many problems, however, scientific progress does not necessarily reduce the model space to a singleton. Instead, |\mathcal M_t|>1 may remain indefinitely. This leads directly to the distinction between identification and underdetermination.

---

5. Identification and Underdetermination

Let the scientific target be represented by T:\Theta\rightarrow\mathcal T. The target need not be the entire model. For example, several physically distinct models may imply the same value of a target property.

The target is point-identified when T(\theta_1)=T(\theta_2) for every \theta_1,\theta_2\in\mathcal M. Equivalently, T(\mathcal M) is a singleton. Partial identification occurs when |T(\mathcal M)|>1. Thus the relevant unresolved object may be not the entire model space but the image

\boxed{T(\mathcal M) = \{T(\theta):\theta\in\mathcal M\}.}

This distinction prevents an important category error. Two models may differ while agreeing on every scientifically relevant target currently under consideration. Therefore \theta_1\neq\theta_2 does not automatically imply unresolvedness with respect to T.

---

6. Contradiction Is Not Underdetermination

A central correction to earlier versions of the methodology is that contradiction and underdetermination must not be treated as the same phenomenon.

6.1 Contradiction

A subset S\subseteq K is inconsistent if \bigcap_{k_j\in S}C_j = \varnothing. A minimal inconsistent subset (MIS) is a set S satisfying \bigcap_{k_j\in S}C_j=\varnothing while every proper subset is consistent. Such structures can be useful for identifying conflicts in a scientific archive. But they do not exist whenever knowledge is underdetermined.

6.2 Underdetermination

Underdetermination occurs when \mathcal M\neq\varnothing and multiple admissible models remain. For a target T, |T(\mathcal M)|>1. No contradiction is required.

6.3 Research Opportunity

A research opportunity requires a third concept. Suppose several models remain admissible: \theta_1,\theta_2,\ldots,\theta_r. The existence of multiple models alone does not tell us what should be investigated next. A research opportunity should therefore involve some operation capable of distinguishing, constraining, or otherwise refining the unresolved structure.

This leads to the central object of this draft.

---

7. Observational Equivalence

Let X denote the current observation family. For two models \theta_1,\theta_2\in\Theta, define observational equivalence under X by

\boxed{\theta_1\sim_X\theta_2 \iff P_{\theta_1}(X\in B) = P_{\theta_2}(X\in B) \quad \forall B.}

Equivalently, when densities exist, p_{\theta_1}(x)=p_{\theta_2}(x) for almost every x. This relation is an equivalence relation under the exact definition. It partitions the model space into equivalence classes [\theta]_X. If two models belong to the same class, the current observation family cannot distinguish them.

This gives a mathematically precise representation of one important form of unresolvedness:

\boxed{\text{unresolved structure} = \text{non-singleton observational equivalence classes}.}

Approximate similarity should not be confused with exact equivalence. For example, a total-variation threshold d_{\mathrm{TV}}(P_{\theta_1},P_{\theta_2})\le\varepsilon does not in general define a transitive equivalence relation. Therefore approximate indistinguishability should be treated as an ambiguity metric or tolerance relation rather than automatically as a quotient relation.

---

8. Research Candidates as Model-Space Refinements

Let c denote a candidate research operation. It may represent an experiment, a measurement, an intervention, a new dataset, a new observational modality, a theoretical constraint, a simulation, a mathematical derivation, or another operation capable of producing additional information.

Let Y_c be the possible result associated with candidate c. For an outcome y, define

\mathcal M_{c,y} = \{\theta\in\mathcal M: P_\theta(Y_c=y)>0\}.

More generally, for continuous variables the corresponding measurable-event formulation is used. The candidate therefore induces a refinement:

\boxed{\mathcal M \longrightarrow \{\mathcal M_{c,y}\}_y.}

The candidate does not necessarily select one model before the experiment occurs. Instead, it creates a set of possible future knowledge states. This distinction is essential.

---

9. The Fundamental Scientific-Discovery Phenomenon

Consider two models \theta_1,\theta_2\in\mathcal M such that \theta_1\sim_X\theta_2. Suppose a candidate c produces an observation Y_c for which

P_{\theta_1}(Y_c\in B) \neq P_{\theta_2}(Y_c\in B)

for some measurable set B. Then the current observation system cannot distinguish the two models, while the candidate can. This is the smallest version of the research-discovery phenomenon proposed here.

---

10. Theorem 1 — Candidate-Induced Refinement

Theorem 1. Let \Theta be a model space and let X be the current observation family. Define

\theta_1\sim_X\theta_2 \iff P_{\theta_1}(X\in B) = P_{\theta_2}(X\in B)

for every measurable B. Let Y_c be the observation generated by candidate c. If \theta_1\sim_X\theta_2 but there exists a measurable set B such that

P_{\theta_1}(Y_c\in B) \neq P_{\theta_2}(Y_c\in B),

then

\boxed{\theta_1\not\sim_{(X,Y_c)}\theta_2.}

Consequently,

\boxed{[\theta_1]_{(X,Y_c)} \subsetneq [\theta_1]_X}

whenever \theta_2\in[\theta_1]_X.

Proof. By assumption, \theta_1\sim_X\theta_2, so P_{\theta_1}(X\in B) = P_{\theta_2}(X\in B) for every measurable event B concerning X. However, there exists a measurable set C concerning Y_c such that P_{\theta_1}(Y_c\in C) \neq P_{\theta_2}(Y_c\in C). Therefore the joint observation family (X,Y_c) cannot have identical distributions under \theta_1 and \theta_2. Hence \theta_1\not\sim_{(X,Y_c)}\theta_2. Since \theta_2 belonged to the original equivalence class [\theta_1]_X but no longer belongs to the corresponding class under the augmented observation family, [\theta_1]_{(X,Y_c)} \subsetneq [\theta_1]_X. The candidate strictly refines the observational equivalence structure. \square

10.1 Scope of Theorem 1 [0.2]

The theorem is one-directional. It says: if a witness pair exists, refinement occurs. It does not say that every refinement has a witness pair, nor that every refinement is scientifically meaningful. This is intentional. The theorem is a primitive, not a characterization.

---

11. Interpretation of Theorem 1

The theorem is intentionally modest. It does not say that every discriminating observation is scientifically important. It does not say that every refinement constitutes a thesis. It does not assign a scalar value to a candidate.

It establishes something more primitive:

\boxed{\text{A research operation can be represented as a refinement of an unresolved model structure.}}

This provides the first mathematical bridge between unresolved scientific problem and candidate research operation. The distinction matters because scientific discovery cannot be reduced to finding contradictions. A perfectly consistent scientific literature can contain substantial underdetermination.

---

12. Identification Gain

Theorem 1 gives a structural notion of refinement. We now seek quantitative measures.

Let T:\Theta\rightarrow\mathcal T be the target of interest. Before candidate c, T(\mathcal M) represents the currently admissible target values. After observing Y_c=y, T(\mathcal M_{c,y}) represents the remaining target values.

A natural structural notion of identification gain is related to the reduction T(\mathcal M) \rightarrow T(\mathcal M_{c,y}). For finite target spaces, one possible measure is cardinality reduction:

G_{\mathrm{card}}(c,y) = |T(\mathcal M)| - |T(\mathcal M_{c,y})|.

For continuous parameter spaces, cardinality is generally useless. Possible alternatives include volume reduction \operatorname{Vol}(T(\mathcal M)) - \operatorname{Vol}(T(\mathcal M_{c,y})), diameter reduction \operatorname{diam}(T(\mathcal M)) - \operatorname{diam}(T(\mathcal M_{c,y})), entropy reduction, or information-theoretic discrimination. No single choice is assumed to be universally correct.

---

13. Information-Theoretic Formulation

Suppose a target random variable T and candidate observation Y_c are defined under a probability model P. The conditional mutual information

I_P(T;Y_c\mid X)

quantifies the expected information about T provided by Y_c beyond X. By the chain rule,

\boxed{I_P(T;X,Y_c) = I_P(T;X) + I_P(T;Y_c\mid X).}

This makes conditional mutual information a natural candidate for measuring incremental information. However, the framework must not assume that information gain is identical to scientific value. A highly informative measurement may be scientifically irrelevant to the actual research target. Conversely, a mathematically small distinction may be scientifically decisive. Therefore

\boxed{\text{information gain} \neq \text{scientific value}}

without additional assumptions.

---

14. Partial Identification and Credal Model Classes

In many real scientific settings, the probability model itself is uncertain. Instead of a single distribution P, we have a set of admissible distributions \mathcal M. This naturally connects the framework to partial identification and credal-set reasoning.

For a candidate c, define

\Delta_c^- = \inf_{P\in\mathcal M} I_P(T;Y_c\mid X)

and

\Delta_c^+ = \sup_{P\in\mathcal M} I_P(T;Y_c\mid X).

Thus the candidate has an information region

\boxed{\Delta_c = [\Delta_c^-,\Delta_c^+].}

The width W_c = \Delta_c^+ - \Delta_c^- measures epistemic uncertainty about the candidate's information value. When H(T)>0, a normalized width can be defined as

\widetilde W_c = \frac{\Delta_c^+-\Delta_c^-}{H(T)}.

14.1 The \Delta_c^- = 0 Pathology [0.2]

It is essential to distinguish two very different conclusions:

· \Delta_c^- > 0 means the candidate carries strictly positive conditional mutual information throughout the admissible model class.
· \Delta_c^- = 0 means that at least one admissible model assigns the candidate zero conditional information. It does not mean that the candidate is valueless.

A candidate with \Delta_c^- = 0 and \Delta_c^+ > 0 is attributionally fragile: its informational value depends entirely on which admissible model is true. A candidate with \Delta_c^- > 0 is attributionally robust. These are different epistemic situations, and the framework must report both endpoints rather than a single number.

This also means that a non-vacuous robust information bound is not automatically a scientific result. It is a statement about the structure of the credal set, not about the importance of the candidate.

---

15. Robust Information Is Not Automatically the Primitive

The robust formulation is attractive because it represents uncertainty about the underlying probability model. But it introduces a deeper question: why should mutual information be the primitive measure of research opportunity?

Other candidates include:

1. Entropy reduction: H(T\mid X)-H(T\mid X,Y_c).
2. Model-space reduction: \mathcal M \rightarrow \mathcal M_{c,y}.
3. Identification gain: T(\mathcal M) \rightarrow T(\mathcal M_{c,y}).
4. Bayesian value of information: \operatorname{VOI}(c).
5. Robust value of information: worst-case or set-valued extension over P\in\mathcal M.
6. Minimum description length: complexity reduction in the surviving explanatory structure.
7. Model discrimination: how many previously indistinguishable models a candidate separates.

These quantities need not agree. Therefore one of the central mathematical questions of the research program is:

\boxed{\text{Which functional, if any, is fundamental to research-opportunity discovery?}}

---

16. Why Scalar Scientific Value Is Problematic

Suppose candidates are evaluated according to

V(c) = f(\text{information}, \text{cost}, \text{generality}, \text{testability}, \text{novelty}, \text{robustness}).

A scalar ranking appears convenient. But different candidates may trade these properties differently. For example, c_1 may have greater identification gain but greater experimental cost, while c_2 may be cheaper but less discriminative. There may be no scientifically justified scalar function that preserves every meaningful preference.

16.1 Pareto Representation [0.2]

Let each candidate be represented by a dimension vector

Q(c) = (G(c), C(c), R(c), F(c), N(c), S(c)),

where the components may represent, for example, identification gain G, cost C, robustness R, falsifiability F, generality N, and structural simplicity S. No universal weighting is imposed.

Definition (Pareto dominance). For candidates c_1,c_2, define

c_1 \succeq c_2 \iff \forall d\in\{G,C,R,F,N,S\}: Q_d(c_1)\ \text{is at least as good as}\ Q_d(c_2),

with the convention that some dimensions are "larger is better" and others are "smaller is better." Define strict dominance c_1\succ c_2 if c_1\succeq c_2 and c_1\not\preceq c_2.

Proposition. \succ is a strict partial order (irreflexive, asymmetric, transitive) on \mathcal C.

Proof. Irreflexivity: c\not\succ c since \succ requires strict improvement on at least one dimension. Asymmetry: if c_1\succ c_2, then c_1 is at least as good on all dimensions and strictly better on at least one; the reverse requires c_2 strictly better on at least one, contradiction. Transitivity: dominance on each dimension is transitive; the conjunction preserves transitivity. \square

The Pareto frontier is

\mathcal P = \{c\in\mathcal C: \nexists c'\in\mathcal C \text{ such that } c'\succ c\}.

The output of the framework is \mathcal P, not a single "best" candidate. This avoids pretending that a universal scientific utility function has already been discovered.

---

17. The Role of Minimal Inconsistent Subsets

Minimal inconsistent subsets remain useful, but their role is now localized. Given constraints K=\{k_1,\ldots,k_m\}, a minimal inconsistent subset is S\subseteq K such that \bigcap_{k\in S}C_k=\varnothing while \bigcap_{k\in S'}C_k\neq\varnothing for every proper subset S'\subset S.

MIS can therefore identify locations where an archive contains incompatible claims. But MIS cannot represent all scientific opportunities. If \mathcal M\neq\varnothing, there may be no MIS at all. Nevertheless, |T(\mathcal M)|>1 may remain large. Thus:

\boxed{\text{MIS detection is one discovery mechanism, not the universal primitive.}}

17.1 Computational Caution [0.2]

Enumeration of all MIS is NP-hard in general. The practical strategy is to bound |S|\le k_{\max} (e.g., k_{\max}=3 or 5) and use SAT/SMT solvers with incremental enumeration. For richer model classes, MDL-gap detection (the smallest additional constraint that would render the knowledge state inconsistent) provides a complementary signal and is often more tractable than exhaustive MIS enumeration.

---

18. The General Discovery Architecture

The resulting architecture becomes:

\boxed{\mathcal A \rightarrow K \rightarrow \mathcal M \rightarrow \text{Unresolved Structures} \rightarrow \text{Candidate Operations} \rightarrow \text{Model Refinement} \rightarrow \text{Validation}.}

The unresolved-structure layer may contain several types:

· Type I: Contradiction. \mathcal M = \varnothing for a subset of constraints.
· Type II: Non-identification. |T(\mathcal M)|>1.
· Type III: Observational equivalence. \theta_1\sim_X\theta_2.
· Type IV: Model disagreement. Different accepted models produce materially different predictions.
· Type V: Boundary failure. A theory works inside one region of parameter space but its validity boundary is unresolved.
· Type VI: Missing mechanism. Multiple observations are known, but the mechanism connecting them remains unspecified.

These structures may generate different classes of research candidates.

---

19. Candidate Generation

A candidate generator should not simply search for keywords such as "gap", "future work", or "open question." Instead, it should search for structural situations such as

\boxed{\text{multiple admissible explanations}},\quad
\boxed{\text{indistinguishable models}},\quad
\boxed{\text{incompatible constraints}},

\boxed{\text{unexplained boundary}},\quad
\boxed{\text{missing mechanism}},

and then ask:

\boxed{\text{What additional operation would discriminate these structures?}}

This changes the discovery problem from textual gap extraction into model-space analysis.

---

20. Minimal-Model Principle

A proposed candidate should first be reduced to the smallest mathematical system in which the claimed phenomenon exists. Let \mathcal M^{(0)} be the smallest admissible model class containing the phenomenon. The candidate survives the minimal-model gate only if the unresolved structure remains present: T(\mathcal M^{(0)}) must remain non-singleton, or the corresponding contradiction/equivalence structure must remain nontrivial.

If the phenomenon disappears under minimalization, the original candidate may depend on unnecessary complexity. This creates a critical research rule:

\boxed{\text{No large thesis before a minimal phenomenon survives.}}

---

21. The Binary Gatekeeper

A useful first mathematical laboratory is a binary system. Let T,X_1,X_2\in\{0,1\}. Their joint distribution belongs to the simplex

\Delta^7 = \left\{p\in\mathbb R^8: p_i\ge0,\; \sum_{i=1}^{8}p_i=1\right\}.

Additional scientific assumptions define an admissible polytope \mathcal M_A\subseteq\Delta^7. For candidate observation X_i, define

\Delta_i^-(S;A) = \inf_{P\in\mathcal M_A} I_P(T;X_i\mid X_S),

and

\Delta_i^+(S;A) = \sup_{P\in\mathcal M_A} I_P(T;X_i\mid X_S).

Then W_i(S;A) = \Delta_i^+(S;A) - \Delta_i^-(S;A). A specification ladder A_0 \rightarrow A_1 \rightarrow A_2 \rightarrow A_3 can be used to test how additional constraints alter the robustness of the inferred information. This provides a concrete environment for counterexamples, optimization, and theorem testing.

21.1 Ladder Design Caution [0.2]

As written in earlier drafts, the ladder A_0 through A_3 constrains the marginals of T and of X_1,X_2 and possibly their correlation, but does not constrain the relationship between T and the instruments. Under such constraints, the minimizing model sets T independent of (X_1,X_2) whenever the marginals are feasible, giving \Delta_i^- = 0 at every rung. The death test then fails for the wrong reason: the ladder is too weak to test the intended hypothesis.

The corrected ladder must include a rung that couples T to the instruments. For the binary toy, one option is a reliability constraint

P(X_i\neq T)\le \epsilon_i,

or a confusion-matrix bound \ell_i(t)\le P(X_i=1\mid T=t)\le u_i(t), with \ell_i(t) bounded away from the marginal P(X_i=1). Without such a rung, the binary gatekeeper does not test non-vacuity of robust information under partial identification; it tests only the trivial fact that mutual information is nonnegative.

---

22. Mathematical Caution: Mutual Information Is Nonlinear

Even when \mathcal M_A is a polytope, the functional P\mapsto I_P(T;X_i\mid X_S) is nonlinear. Therefore its extrema cannot in general be treated as linear programs merely because the feasible region is polyhedral. The optimization problem is

\min_{P\in\mathcal M_A} I_P(T;X_i\mid X_S), \qquad \max_{P\in\mathcal M_A} I_P(T;X_i\mid X_S),

which requires appropriate nonlinear optimization methods. On a finite binary state space, exact computation is feasible via enumeration; for larger spaces, branch-and-bound or interval arithmetic with certificates is required. This correction is methodological, not cosmetic.

---

23. Shapley Attribution as a Secondary Construction

Suppose the observation set is N=\{1,\ldots,n\}. For a fixed probability model P, define v_P(S)=I_P(T;X_S). The Shapley value of observation i is

\phi_i(P) = \sum_{S\subseteq N\setminus\{i\}} \gamma(S)\, I_P(T;X_i\mid X_S),

where

\gamma(S) = \frac{|S|!(n-|S|-1)!}{n!}.

Under model uncertainty,

\Phi_i(\mathcal M) = \{\phi_i(P):P\in\mathcal M\}

is a set-valued attribution. However, Shapley attribution is secondary. It requires the coalition-value function over all relevant subsets. It cannot be replaced by evaluating only singleton observations unless additional structural assumptions are proved. Therefore the discovery framework should not depend on Shapley attribution.

---

24. Kill Operator

Every generated candidate must be exposed to explicit failure conditions.

24.1 Formal Kill Vector [0.2]

Define a kill operator

\mathcal K(c) = (K_{\text{trivial}}, K_{\text{known}}, K_{\text{semantic}}, K_{\text{assumption}}, K_{\text{nonfalsifiable}}, K_{\text{unstable}}, K_{\text{extraction}}),

where each component is 0 (survives) or 1 (killed) with respect to a specified test. The candidate survives iff \mathcal K(c) = \mathbf{0}. The components are:

· K_{\text{trivial}}: the phenomenon disappears under the minimal model.
· K_{\text{known}}: an existing theory already resolves the claimed problem.
· K_{\text{semantic}}: the apparent contradiction is semantic rather than substantive.
· K_{\text{assumption}}: the result depends on an unjustified assumption.
· K_{\text{nonfalsifiable}}: the candidate is not falsifiable.
· K_{\text{unstable}}: the result is unstable under reasonable perturbations.
· K_{\text{extraction}}: the candidate depends on a constraint whose extraction is unreliable.

24.2 Failure as Information

The framework treats failure as information. A killed candidate is not wasted output. It becomes part of the research archive with its kill vector recorded. The set of killed candidates, together with the kill vectors, forms a negative knowledge layer \mathcal K^-(\mathcal A). This layer can itself be analyzed for structural patterns, e.g., recurring assumption failures, recurring semantic confusions, or recurring extraction bottlenecks.

A killed candidate remains retrievable. If a future archive update removes the kill reason, the candidate may be revived. This makes the framework monotone in the right direction: new evidence can un-kill previously killed candidates, but only if the specific kill reason is removed.

---

25. Archive Update

After validation, the knowledge state is updated. If a candidate produces new constraints C_{m+1},C_{m+2},\ldots, then

\mathcal M' = \mathcal M \cap C_{m+1} \cap C_{m+2} \cap\cdots.

Thus the research cycle becomes

\boxed{\mathcal M_t \xrightarrow{\text{candidate}} \mathcal M_{t+1}.}

The process is not simply paper \rightarrow paper. It is:

\boxed{\text{knowledge state} \rightarrow \text{model-space refinement} \rightarrow \text{new knowledge state}.}

---

26. Discovery Pipeline

The complete first-draft pipeline is therefore:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal A &\rightarrow K(\mathcal A) \\
&\rightarrow \mathcal M(\mathcal A) \\
&\rightarrow \{\text{contradictions, ambiguities, non-identification, boundaries}\} \\
&\rightarrow \{\text{candidate operations}\} \\
&\rightarrow \{\mathcal M_{c,y}\}_y \\
&\rightarrow \text{refinement} \\
&\rightarrow \text{identification gain} \\
&\rightarrow \text{validation} \\
&\rightarrow \mathcal A'.
\end{aligned}
}

This is the current candidate architecture for a research-discovery engine.

---

27. A General Research-Opportunity Operator

Let \mathfrak C(\mathcal M) denote the set of candidate operations applicable to the current knowledge state. Define

\mathfrak R: (\mathcal M,c) \mapsto \{\mathcal M_{c,y}\}_y.

Then the discovery problem becomes:

\boxed{\text{Find }c\in\mathfrak C(\mathcal M) \text{ such that } \mathfrak R(\mathcal M,c) \text{ produces scientifically meaningful refinement.}}

The phrase "scientifically meaningful" remains intentionally unresolved. That unresolved definition is not a defect in the draft. It is one of the principal research problems.

---

28. Candidate Evaluation as a Partial Order

Instead of defining an arbitrary scalar score V(c), represent each candidate by a vector

Q(c) = (G(c), C(c), R(c), F(c), N(c), S(c)),

where the components may represent, for example, identification gain G, cost C, robustness R, falsifiability F, generality N, structural simplicity S. No universal weighting is imposed. Candidate comparison is performed through dominance. This produces a Pareto frontier rather than an artificial universal ranking.

28.1 Why a Partial Order and Not a Scalar [0.2]

Any scalarization f: \mathbb R^6 \rightarrow \mathbb R either loses information (some Pareto-dominated candidates receive equal or higher scores than their dominators) or encodes a value judgment about the trade-offs. The value judgment may be defensible, but it must be made explicit and separated from the discovery mechanism. The Pareto frontier keeps the discovery step free of trade-off commitments and returns the trade-off decision to the researcher, where it belongs.

---

29. Historical Backtesting

A major empirical test of the methodology is retrospective. Suppose a historical corpus is truncated at time t_0. Only information available before t_0 is supplied to the discovery system. The system produces candidates C_{t_0}. Later developments \mathcal A_{>t_0} are treated as a holdout. We then ask:

· Did the method recover questions subsequently investigated?
· Did it generate false positives?
· Did it generate candidates judged meaningful by independent experts?
· Did the method identify structures that later became scientifically important?
· How often did its kill operator correctly reject apparently promising but ultimately unproductive candidates?

The key requirement is strict temporal separation. No post-t_0 information may leak into candidate generation.

29.1 Leakage Prevention Protocol [0.2]

Leakage can enter through several channels:

1. Document leakage. Post-t_0 documents in the archive. Mitigation: filter by publication date at the ingestion step.
2. Citation leakage. A pre-t_0 document that cites a post-t_0 result is impossible by construction, but a document may reference an unpublished or in-press result. Mitigation: check references for forward-dated entries.
3. LLM leakage. The LLM may have been trained on post-t_0 text. This is the hardest channel to control. Mitigation: use the LLM only for extraction (archive \rightarrow constraints), not for candidate generation; require every candidate to be traceable to archive constraints via provenance.
4. Evaluator leakage. Human evaluators may know the post-t_0 developments. Mitigation: blind evaluation; evaluators judge candidate meaningfulness, not historical accuracy.
5. Metric leakage. The choice of metrics may be tuned after seeing the ground truth. Mitigation: pre-register metrics before the run.

Without a leakage protocol, historical backtesting produces false confidence. The protocol above is a minimum requirement, not an exhaustive one.

---

30. Negative Controls

Historical backtesting alone can produce false confidence. Therefore negative controls are necessary. Possible controls include:

· shuffled document associations;
· randomized claim graphs;
· semantically matched but structurally irrelevant corpora;
· artificially injected contradictions;
· known closed problems;
· synthetic model spaces with known ground truth.

A method that produces equally impressive candidates from meaningless controls has not demonstrated scientific discovery. It has demonstrated an impressive ability to manufacture academic fog.

---

31. Failure Modes

The framework has several known failure modes.

1. Extraction failure. The archive-to-constraint map E:\mathcal A\rightarrow K may be wrong.
2. Model misspecification. The true model may not belong to \Theta.
3. Missing literature. A supposedly unresolved problem may already have been solved elsewhere.
4. Semantic false contradiction. Two claims may appear contradictory while using different definitions.
5. MIS explosion. The number of minimal inconsistent subsets may become computationally prohibitive.
6. Equivalence undecidability. Exact equivalence may be difficult or impossible to establish in rich model classes.
7. LLM convergence illusion. Agreement among multiple language models does not establish truth.
8. Information-value mismatch. High mutual information does not necessarily imply high scientific importance.
9. Complexity bias. A discovery system may systematically prefer mathematically elegant problems over scientifically important ones.
10. Benchmark contamination. Historical backtesting becomes invalid if later information leaks into the archive or model.
11. Attributional fragility. As noted in Section 14.1, a candidate with \Delta_c^- = 0 may be reported as "non-vacuous" if only \Delta_c^+ is reported. This is a reporting error, not a mathematical one, but it produces spurious results.
12. Epistemic-status collapse. If the epistemic-status layer is omitted, speculative LLM output is silently promoted to supported knowledge, and the entire model space becomes unreliable.

---

32. What This Framework Does Not Claim

This draft does not establish that

\text{scientific discovery} = \text{model-space reduction}.

It does not establish that

\text{research value} = \text{mutual information}.

It does not establish that

\text{scientific tension} = \text{MIS size}.

It does not establish that

\text{LLM agreement} = \text{evidence}.

It does not establish that a Pareto frontier identifies the best scientific problems. It does not establish that an archive can be losslessly converted into formal constraints. These are hypotheses or engineering assumptions requiring separate validation.

---

33. Central Research Question

The framework reduces the enormous original problem to a smaller mathematical question:

\boxed{\textbf{What mathematical object most faithfully represents an unresolved scientific opportunity?}}

The current candidate answer is:

\boxed{\text{an unresolved structure in an admissible model space together with an operation capable of refining it}.}

More formally:

(\mathcal M,\sim_X,c)

where \mathcal M is the admissible model space, \sim_X is the observational-equivalence structure induced by current evidence, and c is a candidate operation whose possible outcomes induce a refinement.

This is deliberately more primitive than a thesis-generation score.

---

34. Research Hypotheses

The draft generates several hypotheses.

H1 — Structural Hypothesis. A substantial class of research opportunities can be represented as unresolved structure in an admissible model space.

H2 — Refinement Hypothesis. A meaningful research question can often be represented as an operation that refines that structure.

H3 — Information Hypothesis. Information-theoretic quantities can quantify some forms of refinement but do not necessarily characterize scientific value completely.

H4 — Robustness Hypothesis. When the probability model is partially identified, set-valued or robust information quantities provide a more faithful representation than a single estimated information value.

H5 — Discovery Hypothesis. A discovery system based on model-space analysis can generate useful candidates without first requiring a predefined textual notion of "research gap."

H6 — Pareto Hypothesis [0.2]. A Pareto frontier over candidate dimensions is more faithful to scientific decision-making than any scalarization, and the frontier itself is stable under reasonable perturbations of the dimension definitions.

H7 — Kill Hypothesis [0.2]. A formal kill operator with recorded reasons produces a negative-knowledge layer that improves future candidate generation by identifying recurring structural failure modes.

These hypotheses require empirical and mathematical testing.

---

35. Proposed Minimal Experiment

Before constructing a large automated system, the framework should be tested on a small domain.

Design. Select approximately 50–200 papers from one well-defined scientific subfield. Construct manually or semi-automatically K(\mathcal A). Translate a small subset into a formal model class \Theta. Construct \mathcal M(\mathcal A). Identify contradictions, non-identification, observational equivalence, and candidate discriminating observations.

Comparison. Compare several candidate functionals: model-space reduction, identification gain, I(T;Y\mid X), \underline I(T;Y\mid X), \overline I(T;Y\mid X), and potentially \operatorname{VOI}.

Objective. The objective is not initially to prove that one metric wins. The objective is to determine whether they capture the same phenomenon or reveal fundamentally different notions of research opportunity.

35.1 Success Criteria [0.2]

The experiment is successful if at least one of the following holds:

1. The Pareto frontier contains a candidate that was not obvious from isolated papers and is independently judged meaningful by a domain expert.
2. The kill operator rejects a candidate that a naive textual method would have accepted, and the rejection reason is judged correct by an independent evaluator.
3. The leakage-controlled backtest recovers at least one post-t_0 development that was not textually anticipated.

The experiment fails if none of these holds, or if the same results are obtained from a shuffled negative control.

35.2 Domain Selection [0.2]

The domain should have: (i) a well-defined literature, (ii) clear constraints extractable from text, (iii) known post-t_0 developments for backtesting, (iv) at least one known "missed" question that the field later resolved. Mars geology, Alzheimer's disease genetics, and high-temperature superconductivity are all candidates. The first is closest to the author's earlier work.

---

36. The First Mathematical Program

The mathematical program following this draft is:

Problem 1. Characterize the relationship between observational equivalence and target identification.

Problem 2. Determine necessary and sufficient conditions under which a candidate strictly refines a target-relevant equivalence class.

Problem 3. Determine when information gain is positive if and only if a candidate provides nontrivial discrimination.

Problem 4. Construct counterexamples showing where these notions diverge.

Problem 5. Characterize robust information gain over compact admissible model classes.

Problem 6. Determine whether a useful partial order over candidate research operations can be constructed without imposing arbitrary scientific preferences.

Problem 7. Determine whether the resulting structures can be detected automatically from scientific archives.

Problem 8 [0.2]. Characterize the computational complexity of MIS enumeration and MDL-gap detection for the model classes that arise in practice.

Problem 9 [0.2]. Determine sufficient conditions under which the Pareto frontier is stable under small perturbations of the dimension definitions and the admissible model class.

---

37. Second Theorem Target

A possible strengthening of Theorem 1 is:

Theorem 2 (Strict Target Identification Under Candidate Observation). Let T:\Theta\rightarrow\mathcal T be a target function. Suppose there exist \theta_1,\theta_2\in\mathcal M such that T(\theta_1)\neq T(\theta_2) while \theta_1\sim_X\theta_2. If candidate c distinguishes \theta_1 and \theta_2 through Y_c, then under suitable regularity conditions the augmented observation family can strictly reduce the target identification region.

A rigorous version must specify precisely what "distinguishes" and "strictly reduce" mean for the target space. This theorem is intentionally left as a target rather than presented as established.

37.1 What "Strictly Reduce" Should Mean [0.2]

The natural candidate is not cardinality reduction of T(\mathcal M), because T(\mathcal M) can be infinite. The correct notion is likely one of:

· Set-theoretic reduction: T(\mathcal M_{(X,Y_c)}) \subsetneq T(\mathcal M_X).
· Diameter reduction: \operatorname{diam}(T(\mathcal M_{(X,Y_c)})) < \operatorname{diam}(T(\mathcal M_X)).
· Measure reduction: \mu(T(\mathcal M_{(X,Y_c)})) < \mu(T(\mathcal M_X)) for a specified reference measure \mu.

The theorem must state which notion is used, and the proof must establish the corresponding inequality.

---

38. The Discovery Loop

The final methodological loop becomes:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\text{Archive} &\rightarrow \text{Constraints} \\
&\rightarrow \text{Admissible Models} \\
&\rightarrow \text{Unresolved Structure} \\
&\rightarrow \text{Candidate Operation} \\
&\rightarrow \text{Minimal Model} \\
&\rightarrow \text{Theorem / Counterexample} \\
&\rightarrow \text{Kill Test} \\
&\rightarrow \text{Validation} \\
&\rightarrow \text{Updated Knowledge State}.
\end{aligned}
}

The important difference from the original iterative methodology is that the loop cannot continue merely because a new conceptual gap has appeared. Progress requires one of:

\boxed{\text{proof} \quad\text{or}\quad \text{counterexample} \quad\text{or}\quad \text{validated minimal phenomenon}.}

A new framework cannot be introduced merely to avoid killing the previous one.

---

39. Preliminary Conclusion

This draft proposes a mathematical reframing of scientific research discovery. The starting point is not the thesis. It is the knowledge state. The knowledge state induces an admissible model space: \mathcal A \rightarrow \mathcal M(\mathcal A).

Unresolvedness is then represented through non-identification, observational equivalence, contradiction, model disagreement, or related structures. A candidate research operation is represented as a mechanism capable of refining the admissible model space:

\mathcal M \rightarrow \{\mathcal M_{c,y}\}_y.

The first theorem establishes the simplest version of this phenomenon: a candidate observation that distinguishes models previously observationally equivalent necessarily refines the corresponding equivalence class.

Information theory, robust inference, partial identification, MIS, VOI, MDL, and Pareto analysis can then be investigated as possible tools for quantifying, discovering, or prioritizing such structures. The framework deliberately stops short of declaring any one of them fundamental.

The central unresolved question remains:

\boxed{\textit{What is the mathematically correct primitive of research opportunity?}}

The answer must survive minimal models, counterexamples, formal proofs, empirical backtesting, and comparison with existing scientific-discovery frameworks. Only after that survival test should the framework be promoted from a methodology to a theory.

