Appendix A — Formal Proofs

A.1 Proof of Proposition 2.1 (Epistemic-Status Propagation is Well-Defined)

Statement. Let (\mathcal S, \preceq) be the epistemic-status lattice with

\mathcal S = \{\text{verified}, \text{supported}, \text{hypothetical}, \text{speculative}, \text{contradicted}, \text{killed}, \text{unresolved}\}

ordered by

\text{verified} \succ \text{supported} \succ \text{hypothetical} \succ \text{speculative}.

If a claim k is derived from \{k_{j_1},\ldots,k_{j_r}\} with statuses \sigma(k_{j_i}), then the induced status

\sigma(k) := \min_{i} \sigma(k_{j_i})

is well-defined and independent of the order of derivation.

Proof. The minimum is taken in the total preorder \preceq. Since \preceq is total on the four core statuses, the minimum exists and is unique up to equality. Order-independence follows from commutativity of \min in a total preorder. \square

Remark. The three remaining statuses — contradicted, killed, unresolved — are not on the same axis. Contradicted and killed are terminal; unresolved is a marker for candidates awaiting validation. The propagation rule applies only to the four graded statuses; terminal statuses are set by the kill operator (Appendix D).

---

A.2 Proof of Theorem 1 (Candidate-Induced Refinement)

Statement. Let \Theta be a model space and let X be the current observation family. Define

\theta_1 \sim_X \theta_2 \iff P_{\theta_1}(X\in B) = P_{\theta_2}(X\in B) \quad \forall B.

Let Y_c be the observation generated by candidate c. If \theta_1 \sim_X \theta_2 and there exists a measurable set B such that

P_{\theta_1}(Y_c \in B) \neq P_{\theta_2}(Y_c \in B),

then \theta_1 \not\sim_{(X,Y_c)} \theta_2, and consequently [\theta_1]_{(X,Y_c)} \subsetneq [\theta_1]_X.

Proof. Suppose \theta_1 \sim_X \theta_2. Then for every measurable B \subseteq \mathcal X we have P_{\theta_1}(X\in B) = P_{\theta_2}(X\in B).

The joint family (X,Y_c) has a joint law P_\theta(X\in \cdot, Y_c\in \cdot). Consider the cylinder event

E = \mathcal X \times B_c

for the witness set B_c \subseteq \mathcal Y_c. Then

P_{\theta_1}((X,Y_c)\in E) = P_{\theta_1}(Y_c \in B_c),

P_{\theta_2}((X,Y_c)\in E) = P_{\theta_2}(Y_c \in B_c).

By the witness property these are unequal. Hence \theta_1 \not\sim_{(X,Y_c)} \theta_2.

Since \theta_2 \in [\theta_1]_X (equivalence class under X) and \theta_2 \notin [\theta_1]_{(X,Y_c)}, we have

[\theta_1]_{(X,Y_c)} \subseteq [\theta_1]_X, \quad \text{properly}.

The inclusion is proper because \theta_2 is in the right-hand side but not the left. \square

Corollary. If no such witness pair exists, then [\theta_1]_{(X,Y_c)} = [\theta_1]_X for all \theta_1, i.e., candidate c produces no refinement at the level of observational equivalence classes.

---

A.3 Proof of Proposition 16.1 (Pareto Order is a Strict Partial Order)

Statement. Define c_1 \succeq c_2 iff for all dimensions d \in \{G,C,R,F,N,S\},

Q_d(c_1) \text{ is at least as good as } Q_d(c_2),

with orientation conventions fixed per dimension. Define c_1 \succ c_2 iff c_1 \succeq c_2 and c_2 \not\succeq c_1. Then \succ is irreflexive, asymmetric, and transitive.

Proof.

Irreflexivity. Suppose c \succ c. Then c \succeq c (trivially true) and c \not\succeq c (required for the strictness), a contradiction. Hence c \not\succ c.

Asymmetry. Suppose c_1 \succ c_2. Then c_1 \succeq c_2 and c_2 \not\succeq c_1. If also c_2 \succ c_1, then c_2 \succeq c_1, contradicting c_2 \not\succeq c_1. Hence c_2 \not\succ c_1.

Transitivity. Suppose c_1 \succ c_2 and c_2 \succ c_3. Then:

· c_1 \succeq c_2 and c_2 \succeq c_3 imply c_1 \succeq c_3 (dominance is componentwise and hence transitive).
· c_3 \not\succeq c_1 because if c_3 \succeq c_1, then c_3 \succeq c_1 \succeq c_2 \succeq c_3, so c_3 \succeq c_2, contradicting c_2 \succ c_3 (which requires c_3 \not\succeq c_2).

Hence c_1 \succ c_3. \square

Remark. The Pareto frontier \mathcal P = \{c : \nexists c' \text{ with } c' \succ c\} is nonempty whenever \mathcal C is finite and nonempty, by a standard ascending-chain argument on the partial order.

---

A.4 Proof of Proposition 14.1 (\Delta_c^- = 0 is not equivalent to attributional vacuity)

Statement. Let \Delta_c^- = \inf_{P \in \mathcal M} I_P(T;Y_c \mid X) and \Delta_c^+ = \sup_{P \in \mathcal M} I_P(T;Y_c \mid X). Then:

(a) \Delta_c^- = 0 does not imply that I_P(T;Y_c\mid X) = 0 for all P \in \mathcal M.

(b) \Delta_c^- = 0 is compatible with \Delta_c^+ > 0.

(c) \Delta_c^- > 0 implies I_P(T;Y_c\mid X) > 0 for all P \in \mathcal M.

Proof.

(a) The infimum equals 0 if there exists P_0 \in \mathcal M with I_{P_0}(T;Y_c\mid X) = 0, or if a sequence approaches 0. Either way, other P \in \mathcal M may have positive value.

(b) Choose \mathcal M = \{P_0, P_1\} with I_{P_0} = 0 and I_{P_1} = c > 0. Then \Delta_c^- = 0, \Delta_c^+ = c.

(c) Immediate from definition of infimum and nonnegativity of CMI. \square

---

A.5 Proof of Proposition 3.1 (Compactness of Finite Polytope)

Statement. If \mathcal M_A \subseteq \Delta^{K-1} is defined by finitely many linear equalities and inequalities and is nonempty, then \mathcal M_A is compact.

Proof. The simplex \Delta^{K-1} is closed and bounded in \mathbb R^K, hence compact (Heine–Borel). \mathcal M_A is the intersection of \Delta^{K-1} with finitely many closed half-spaces and hyperplanes, hence closed in \Delta^{K-1}. A closed subset of a compact set is compact. \square

---

A.6 Proof of Theorem 3.3 (Existence of Extrema)

Statement. If \mathcal M_A is nonempty and compact, and P \mapsto I_P(T;X_i\mid X_S) is continuous on \Delta^{K-1}, then \Delta_i^-(S;A) and \Delta_i^+(S;A) exist and are attained.

Proof. Continuity of I_P on the finite simplex under the convention 0\log 0 = 0 is a standard result. A continuous real-valued function on a nonempty compact set attains its infimum and supremum (Weierstrass). \square

