Appendix C — Specification Ladder: General Framework

C.1 Definition of a Specification Ladder

A specification ladder is a pre-registered, monotone sequence of assumption sets

A_0 \subset A_1 \subset \cdots \subset A_m

where each A_k is a set of constraints on the admissible model class \mathcal M_A.

C.2 Monotonicity of Bounds

Proposition. If A_k \subset A_{k+1}, then

\mathcal M_{A_{k+1}} \subseteq \mathcal M_{A_k},

and consequently

\Delta_i^-(S;A_k) \le \Delta_i^-(S;A_{k+1}) \le \Delta_i^+(S;A_{k+1}) \le \Delta_i^+(S;A_k).

Proof. The infimum over a smaller set is at least as large; the supremum over a smaller set is at most as small. \square

Consequence. The identification width W_i(S;A_k) is non-increasing in k.

C.3 Assumption-Sensitivity Curve

For each (i,S), plot \Delta_i^-(S;A_k), \Delta_i^+(S;A_k), and W_i(S;A_k) against k.

The interpretation:

· A flat curve means the bound is insensitive to the assumption.
· A curve that moves from vacuous to non-vacuous identifies the rung where non-vacuity is restored.
· A curve that remains vacuous at all k means the framework fails for this (i,S).

C.4 Rung Defensibility Audit

For each rung A_k, record in writing:

1. The scientific justification for the constraint.
2. The provenance of the constraint (which papers, which measurements, which theoretical results).
3. The cost of the constraint: what admissible models are excluded, and why.
4. The falsifiability: what observation would invalidate the constraint.

The audit is part of the deliverable. A rung without a written audit is not admissible.

C.5 Circularity Prohibition

The ladder must be fixed before computing any bounds. If a rung is added after seeing the results, the exercise is invalid. The pre-registration document (Appendix B.6) and the final report must be deposited together.

