Appendix F — Literature-Collision Protocol

F.1 Objective

Determine whether a candidate c is already known, under a different name, in a different field, or in a different formulation.

F.2 Protocol

Step 1 — Formal extraction. Extract the formal structure of c:

\text{Form}(c) = (\mathcal P, \mathcal M_c, \mathcal C_c, \mathcal O_c, \mathcal H),

where \mathcal P is premises, \mathcal M_c admissible models, \mathcal C_c constraints, \mathcal O_c observations, \mathcal H unresolved hypotheses.

Step 2 — Query construction. Construct a structured query over the formal structure, not keywords. For example:

· "Any work where premises \mathcal P and constraints \mathcal C_c produce admissible models \mathcal M_c containing multiple equivalence classes under observation family \mathcal O_c?"

Step 3 — Structural matching. For each returned paper, compare formal objects under renaming. Two formal structures are equivalent if there is a bijection between the symbols that preserves all relations.

Step 4 — Verdict. If an equivalent structure is found: K_{\text{known}} = 1. If a partially equivalent structure is found (some components match, others differ): record the partial match and continue.

F.3 Failure Cases

· Lexical mismatch. The existing work uses different terminology. Mitigation: use structural matching, not keyword matching.
· Field mismatch. The existing work is in a different field. Mitigation: search across fields using structural queries.
· Undecidability. Structural equivalence in rich formal languages is undecidable. Mitigation: restrict to decidable fragments (propositional logic, linear arithmetic, finite model spaces).

F.4 Negative Result

If no equivalent structure is found, the candidate is marked "novel up to the search scope" — not "novel" absolutely. The search scope must be recorded.

