Appendix G — Historical Backtesting: Full Protocol

G.1 Design

Fix a cutoff time t_0. Split the archive:

\mathcal A = \mathcal A_{\le t_0} \cup \mathcal A_{> t_0}.

Run the discovery pipeline on \mathcal A_{\le t_0}. Produce candidate set \mathcal C_{t_0}.

Evaluate against \mathcal A_{> t_0}.

G.2 Leakage Prevention

Channel 1 — Document leakage. Filter all documents by publication date \le t_0. Remove any document whose publication date is unknown or after t_0.

Channel 2 — Citation leakage. Scan references of \mathcal A_{\le t_0} for forward-dated entries. Remove or flag.

Channel 3 — LLM leakage. The LLM may have been trained on post-t_0 text. Mitigation: use the LLM only for extraction, not for candidate generation. Every candidate must be traceable via provenance to constraints extracted from \mathcal A_{\le t_0}.

Channel 4 — Evaluator leakage. Human evaluators may know post-t_0 developments. Mitigation: blind evaluation; evaluators judge candidate meaningfulness independent of historical accuracy.

Channel 5 — Metric leakage. Pre-register all metrics before the run. Do not tune thresholds after seeing results.

Channel 6 — Prompt leakage. The prompt to any LLM must not contain post-t_0 information. Audit prompts.

G.3 Metrics

Rediscovery rate. Fraction of post-t_0 developments in \mathcal A_{> t_0} that appear as candidates in \mathcal C_{t_0}.

Precision. Fraction of candidates in \mathcal C_{t_0} judged meaningful by independent evaluators.

False-positive rate. Fraction of candidates judged novel but trivial.

Kill accuracy. Fraction of killed candidates that were genuinely unresolvable.

Reproducibility. Jaccard overlap between candidate sets from independent runs.

Convergence speed. Number of iterations before a stable candidate set emerges.

Negative-control signal. Fraction of candidates that also appear when the archive is shuffled. If this fraction is high, the method is producing noise.

G.4 Negative Controls

Construct the following control archives:

1. Shuffled. Randomly shuffle which claims come from which documents.
2. Randomized. Replace claims with random claims of the same epistemic status.
3. Irrelevant. Replace the archive with a semantically matched but structurally irrelevant corpus.
4. Injected-contradiction. Add artificial contradictions to a consistent archive.
5. Known-closed. Use an archive in which all problems are known to have been solved.

A method that produces equally impressive candidates from these controls has not demonstrated discovery.

G.5 Reporting

Report for each metric:

· Point estimate.
· Confidence interval (bootstrap or Bayesian).
· Comparison to negative controls.

Report for each candidate:

· The provenance set.
· The kill vector.
· The Pareto position.
· The independent evaluator's judgment.

G.6 Known Limitations

· Historical rediscovery does not imply future predictive power.
· A method can rediscover known results while failing on genuinely novel problems.
· The evaluator's judgment is itself a source of uncertainty.

