Appendix H — Minimal Experiment: Full Specification

H.1 Domain Selection

Criteria:

1. Well-defined literature, 50–200 papers.
2. Constraints extractable from text (quantitative or logical).
3. Known post-t_0 developments for backtesting.
4. At least one known "missed" question that the field later resolved.

Candidate domains:

· Mars geology (Margin Unit).
· Alzheimer's disease genetics.
· High-temperature superconductivity.
· Exoplanet atmospheric chemistry.

H.2 Claim Extraction

For each paper, extract:

· Problems stated.
· Hypotheses proposed.
· Methods used.
· Results claimed.
· Concepts introduced.
· Questions left open.

Each extraction carries provenance, confidence, and epistemic status.

H.3 Model-Class Construction

Select a subset of claims that can be expressed as constraints on a common parameter space \Theta. For Mars geology, \Theta might include:

· Alteration temperature T \in [0, 500] °C.
· Fluid composition (pH, salinity).
· Timing (relative order of episodes).
· Mineral assemblage.

For a 50-paper corpus, aim for 5–10 parameters and 20–50 constraints.

H.4 Tension Detection

Run:

· MIS enumeration with k_{\max} = 3.
· MDL-gap detection with candidate constraints drawn from the parameter space.
· Observational-equivalence class computation for the target T.

H.5 Candidate Generation

For each tension hotspot, generate the minimal phenomenon and test it on a toy model. Classify as KILL / WEAK / INTERESTING / STRONG.

H.6 Value-of-Information Computation

For each surviving candidate, compute:

· \Delta_c^- and \Delta_c^+ over the admissible model class.
· W_c and \widetilde W_c.
· \operatorname{VOI}(c) if a utility function can be justified.

H.7 Pareto Ranking

Compute the dimension vector for each surviving candidate. Return the Pareto frontier.

H.8 Evaluation

Compare against:

· Post-t_0 developments (backtest).
· Independent expert judgment (blinded).
· Negative controls (shuffled, randomized, irrelevant).

H.9 Success Criteria

The experiment is successful if at least one of:

1. The Pareto frontier contains a candidate judged meaningful by an independent expert that was not obvious from isolated papers.
2. The kill operator rejects a candidate that a naive method would have accepted, and the rejection is judged correct.
3. The backtest recovers at least one post-t_0 development not textually anticipated.

The experiment fails if:

· No candidate survives the kill operator.
· All surviving candidates are judged trivial.
· Negative controls produce comparable candidates.

H.10 Resource Estimate

· Extraction: 2–4 weeks for 50–200 papers with LLM assistance.
· Model-class construction: 1–2 weeks.
· Tension detection: 1 week (SMT solver).
· Candidate generation: 1–2 weeks.
· Evaluation: 2–4 weeks (expert time).
· Total: 2–3 months.

