Appendix I — Literature Map: Extended

I.1 Literature-Based Discovery (LBD)

Key works. Swanson (1986); Smalheiser & Swanson (1994); modern computational extensions (Wimmelbücker et al., 2020; Sebastiao et al., 2023).

What it does. Detects A–B–C links across disjoint literatures.

What it misses. No formal tension concept; no kill mechanism; no model-space representation.

Overlap with this framework. The ABC model is a special case of cross-domain connection discovery. The framework generalizes it by adding formal structure and validation.

I.2 Scientific Knowledge Graphs

Key works. ORKG (Jaradeh et al., 2019); HyGRAIL (Liu et al., 2023); HypoChainer (Wang et al., 2024).

What it does. Structured representation of scientific claims; link prediction.

What it misses. Gap detection is treated as link prediction, not as scientific tension; no partial identification; no value-of-information.

Overlap. The typed heterogeneous graph representation is shared. The tension-as-MIS formulation is new relative to this literature.

I.3 Automated Scientific Discovery

Key works. Co-Scientist (Google, 2025); AutoSciDACT (2024); HypoEvolve (2024).

What it does. Hypothesis generation; anomaly detection.

What it misses. No formal stopping rule; novelty metrics are coarse; no robust-information framework.

Overlap. The pipeline structure is shared. The kill operator and Pareto ranking are additions.

I.4 Value of Information (VoI)

Key works. Howard (1966); Yokota & Thompson (2004); PCORI VOI framework (2012).

What it does. Quantifies expected reduction in loss from future data.

What it misses. Applied to parameter estimation, not to research problem selection.

Overlap. The robust CMI formulation extends VoI to the partially identified case.

I.5 Partial Identification

Key works. Manski (1990, 2003); Tamer (2010); Jiang & Kocaoglu (2024).

What it does. Bounds on causal effects under unmeasured confounding.

What it misses. Not connected to scientific discovery.

Overlap. The admissible model space \mathcal M_A is a partial-identification set. The contribution is connecting it to discovery.

I.6 Imprecise Probability

Key works. Walley (1991); Augustin et al. (2014); Moral (2024).

What it does. Credal sets; lower/upper probabilities; robust Bayesian inference.

What it misses. Not applied to scientific discovery.

Overlap. The framework uses credal sets to represent \mathcal M_A.

I.7 Formal Epistemology

Key works. Lipton (2004); Douven (2021); Schupbach (2018).

What it does. Abduction; inference to the best explanation; explanationism.

What it misses. Not computational.

Overlap. The minimal-phenomenon principle is a computational version of IBE.

I.8 Multi-Objective Optimization

Key works. Deb (2001); Miettinen (1999); Ehrgott (2005).

What it does. Pareto optimality; non-dominated sorting.

What it misses. Not applied to scientific candidate ranking.

Overlap. The Pareto formulation is a direct application.

I.9 Causal Discovery

Key works. Pearl (2009); Spirtes et al. (2000); Peters et al. (2017).

What it does. Causal structure learning from data.

What it misses. Requires interventional data; not applicable to archive-based discovery without interventional sources.

Overlap. The observational-equivalence framework is shared with causal discovery.

I.10 Algorithmic Information Theory

Key works. Rissanen (1978); Li & Vitányi (2008); Grünwald (2007).

What it does. MDL; Kolmogorov complexity; model selection.

What it misses. Compression is not truth; compact false theories exist.

Overlap. MDL-gap detection is a specific use of MDL for tension detection.

